+# BLOCKED: the 1 ms SLA in `test_sla` is not physically reachable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the reference corpus in **under
+1 ms**:
+
+```python
+text = corpus()                       # 4,083,137 bytes, 600,000 words
+...
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+The corpus is generated inside the test from a fixed seed, so it cannot be
+memoized, pre-indexed, or computed ahead of time. The timer wraps only the
+`top_words()` call.
+
+The correctness half of `test_sla` **passes**: `top_words()` returns exactly
+the same list as the test's `Counter`/`sorted` reference. Only the timing
+assertion fails.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+`elapsed < 1 ms` for a 4 MB input means the whole pipeline must average better
+than ~4 GB/s, in CPython 3.12.5, while doing per-word Python work. Measured on
+this machine (best of 5 runs, same corpus the test builds):
+
+| Stage                                                | Time |
+|------------------------------------------------------|------|
+| `text.lower()` (**input must at least be normalized**) | 1.49 ms |
+| `text.lower().split()` (cheapest tokenization available) | 21.3 ms |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` (contract tokenization) | 62.3 ms |
+| `Counter(...)` of already-tokenized words             | 32.8 ms |
+| sorting the ~4,776 distinct words and slicing the top 10 | 1.80 ms |
+| **current `top_words()`**                             | **100.4 ms** |
+| even a contract-breaking `split()`-only pipeline      | 58.6 ms |
+
+The decisive point: **lowercasing alone takes 1.49 ms**, already ~1.5x over
+the entire SLA, before tokenizing or counting a single word. There is no
+implementation of the documented contract ("runs of letters and apostrophes,
+compared case-insensitively") that can skip that step and still be correct.
+
+* Counting all 600,000 tokens is unavoidable: correct top-10 results require
+  every occurrence (a word just outside the top 10 can move into it), so
+  sampling or early exit is not an option.
+* Caching by input identity does not help: the test makes exactly one timed
+  call, and the corpus is freshly generated each run. (CPython caches
+  `hash(text)` on the string, but that only makes the cache *lookup* free; it
+  does not do the work.)
+* The fastest measured alternative — `Counter(text.lower().split())`, which
+  would mishandle punctuation in real articles and violate the docstring — is
+  still 58.6 ms, i.e. **~59x the SLA**.
+* Meeting 1 ms in a language runtime would require a single-pass native
+  tokenizer and counter operating at close to memory bandwidth. Even then,
+  the 4 GB/s implied by the SLA is at the edge of what a CPU can sustain for
+  read + lowercase + hash + count, and it is not achievable from Python
+  without a compiled extension. No such extension exists in this project, and
+  adding/building one would be a workaround rather than a fix.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`wordfreq.py` was fixed for the real performance problem: the old code did
+`w in seen` plus `seen.index(w)` (linear list scans) for every token, i.e.
+O(tokens x vocabulary). It now uses `collections.Counter` and a precompiled
+regex:
+
+```python
+counts = Counter(_WORD_RE.findall(text.lower()))
+return sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda p: (-p[1], p[0]))[:n]
+```
+
+On a 60,000-word corpus this is ~45x faster (532 ms -> 11.7 ms); on the full
+reference corpus it is ~100 ms instead of many seconds. Behaviour is
+unchanged: case-insensitive, regex tokenization, alphabetical tie-break.
+
+Test results with `python3 -m unittest test_wordfreq`:
+
+- `test_basic` — pass
+- `test_case_and_apostrophes` — pass
+- `test_ties_alphabetical` — pass
+- `test_sla` — **fails only on `assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)`** (actual
+  `~0.10 s`, best observed `~0.098 s`); its equality assertion passes.
+
+The test was intentionally **not** weakened, skipped, or re-timed, and no
+attempt was made to fake the measurement.
+
+## What would unblock this
+
+1. Change the SLA to a realistic budget for CPython (e.g. 150 ms for 4 MB),
+   or state it per kilobyte; or
+2. Move `top_words` behind a compiled extension / native library that
+   tokenizes and counts in one pass. Even a good native implementation is
+   likely to land in the low single-digit milliseconds, not 1 ms; or
+3. Redefine the API so an index is built once per document and queries are
+   cheap, instead of counting from raw text on every call.
